readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|<poem>The desperate tempest hath so ''bang’d'' the Turks
That their designment halts.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>What galleys have we ''bang’d'', and sunk, and taken,
Whose only fraughts were fire and stern defiance.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Double Marriage, ii. 1.}}}}
'''3.''' To produce a loud noise from or by, as in slamming a door, and the like: as, he went out and ''banged'' the door behind him.
{{smaller block|Twa unlucky redcoats . . . ''banged'' aff a gun at him.
{{right|''Scott'', Waverley, II. xxviii.}}}}
'''4.''' To beat in any quality or action; surpass; excel. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|The practical denial of the common brotherhood of the same family ''bangs'' heathenism.
{{right|''J. Mill.''}}}}
{{smaller block|That ''bangs'' Banagher, and Banagher ''bangs'' the world.
{{right|''Irish saying.''}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To strike violently or noisily; thump: usually with ''against.''
{{smaller block|Now there are certain particles or small masses of matter which we know to ''bang'' against one another according to certain laws.
{{right|''W. K. Clifford'', Lectures, I. 177.}}}}
'''2.''' To resound with clashing noises.
{{smaller block|<poem>The maid and page renew’d their strife,
The palace ''bang’d'' and buzz’d and clackt.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Day-Dream.}}}}
'''3.''' To spring or move with sudden energy or impetus; bounce: as, he ''banged'' up at once.

'''bang<sup>1</sup>''' (bang), ''n.'' [= Icel. ''bang'' = Sw. ''bång'', a hammering, = Norw. Dan. ''bank'', a beating; from the verb.] '''1.''' A heavy, resounding blow; a thump, as with a club.
{{smaller block|<poem>The very first blow that the forester gave,
He made his broad weapon cry twang;</poem>}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>’Twas over the head, he fell down for dead,
O, that was a damnable ''bang!''</poem>
{{right|''Robin Hood and the Ranger'', in Child’s Ballads, V. 209.}}}}
{{smaller block|I heard several ''bangs'' or buffets . . . given to the eagle that held the ring of my box in his beak.
{{right|''Swift'', Gulliver’s Travels.}}}}
'''2.''' A loud, sudden, explosive noise, as the discharge of a gun or cannon, the slamming of a door, etc.
{{smaller block|The steps of a fine-belozenged carriage were let down with a ''bang.'' {{right|''Thackeray'', Newcomes, II.}}}}
'''3.''' A sudden, impetuous movement; an energetic dash or bounce: as, he got up with a ''bang.'' —'''4.''' A stick; a club. [North. Eng.]='''Syn. 1.''' See ''thump.''

'''bang<sup>1</sup>''' (bang), ''adv.'' [Adverbial use of ''bang''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'' or ''n.''] With a sudden or violent blow or clap; all of a sudden; abruptly: especially with ''come'' or ''go:'' as, ''bang went'' the guns.
{{smaller block|A 32lb. shot struck us ''bang'' on the quarter.
{{right|''Tom Cringle’s Log'', Blackwood’s Mag., XXXII. 31.}}}}

'''bang<sup>2</sup>''' (bang), ''v. t.'' [< ''bang''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.''; to cut the hair ‘bang off.’] To cut across: used of hair. (''a'') To cut (the hair) so as to form a fringe over the forehead: a common fashion with girls and young women.

